## Project Structure (required)


My_Directory_Structure/
├── client/                          # Frontend React SPA
│   └── src/
│       ├── components/
│       ├── hooks/                   # React Query hooks (split by feature)
│       ├── lib/
│       └── pages/
├── server/                          # Backend Node.js/Express server
│   ├── ai/                          # Modular AI logic directory
│   │   ├── chains/                  # Orchestrates multi-call AI sequences
│   │   │   ├── action-plan.chain.ts
│   │   │   ├── chat-refinement.chain.ts
│   │   │   └── purpose-discovery.chain.ts
│   │   ├── prompts.ts               # Manages system prompt generation & persona
│   │   ├── schemas.ts               # Zod/OpenAPI schemas for AI validation
│   │   ├── tools.ts                 # Function-calling tool definitions
│   │   ├── types.ts                 # TypeScript types for the Gemini API
│   │   └── wrapper.ts               # Low-level Gemini API client wrapper
│   ├── routes/                      # Feature-based API route handlers
│   │   ├── assessment.ts            # Handles /analyze and /action-plan
│   │   └── chat.ts                  # Handles /chat
│   ├── cache.ts                     # In-memory cache implementation
│   └── storage.ts                   # In-memory session storage
├── shared/                          # Isomorphic code
│   └── schema.ts                    # Drizzle/Zod schemas
└── .env.example


> **Naming convention:** Keep **kebab-case** for all files & folders (e.g., `question-card.tsx`).
> Use absolute imports with the `@` alias (already configured in `vite.config.ts`).

---

## Environment Variables

- **Backend-only** variables live in `.env.local` (never commit to VCS).
- **No values are exposed to the browser** because the client is a pure SPA hitting our own Express API.
- If you must expose a variable to the client, prefix it with `VITE_` (Vite's convention) – but avoid this when possible.
- Update `.env.example` whenever you add or rename a variable.

---

## Type Rules

- All global/shared types go in `shared/` or `client/src/types/`.
- Use barrel files (`index.ts`) for exporting, following the existing project pattern.
- Prefer **interfaces** for defining object shapes.

---

## “Action State” Helper Pattern

We use a generic type for consistent API function results:

```ts
export type ActionState<T> =
  | { isSuccess: true; message: string; data: T }
  | { isSuccess: false; message: string; data?: never }
This should be returned from any helper functions you add under server/ or future storage helpers.